 "relevance_note": "Single-patient case report of a clinically diagnosed infant: 'tortuous pulmonary arteries with multiple peripheral pulmonary stenoses and bilateral inguinal hernia pointing towards the diagnosis of arterial tortuosity syndrome'; no SLC2A10 testing is reported.",
 "method_note": "Read the whole paper by hand; no table transcribed by script. One in-scope person (the unnamed 5-month-old boy, label 'patient'), diagnosed clinically/angiographically; no genetic testing or SLC2A10 variant is reported, so the variants table is empty and n_patients_reported (patients with a SLC2A10 variant) is null. The Wahab et al series in the Discussion is summarised only as counts (no individual-level facts), so it gets no rows; references [2-4] are cited only generically. 'Normal facies and skin texture' recorded as excluded 'abnormal facies' and 'abnormal skin texture' (D-27); it was not used to exclude the narrower checklist items (down-slanting palpebral fissures, lax skin). 'Lacked all the physical features characteristic of this syndrome' recorded as a feature row as stated; it was not used to exclude hyperextensible skin or joint hypermobility, because the referent is set two paragraphs earlier in a general description. Catheter pressures (Table 1: RV 110/15, main PA 100/30) and TR jet >4 m/s were not converted to 'pulmonary hypertension', which the paper never names for this patient. 'Fast breathing' was recorded as a feature but not as checklist 'respiratory distress or insufficiency'. Vital status left not reported (only 'discharged on medical follow-up'). No contradictions found.",
